>      Write fault error writing device AUX

MS-DOS considers AUX, PRN, CON, LPT1 and several other names to be
devices.  Try creating a file called AUX some time.  In fact, even
those names with extensions won't serve as ordinary files.  Anyway
see if there is a file or directory called AUX.  You might have to
use some trickery to rename it, too!

My stepdad tripped this trap twice.  He once called me up because he
was trying to create a file called AUX on his DOS box.  On another
occasion, he called to ask why he couldn't execute his unix shell
script "test" by typing "test".  ("test" was a builtin command in
the shell he was using.)

Moral: name all files as randomly and nondescriptively as possible.
Corrolary: name program variables the same way to avoid clashes with
  reserved words.  If it were easy to understand, it wouldn't be
  called "code", right?  <grin>

